// fuzz-mutate-08: stmt-permute's open-cell guard sees only a bare `never()`;
// a let over a wrapped bottom (or `[]`) still has its two readers swapped
// command: graphix-fuzz typemorph design/review-2026-10-05/repro/fuzz-mutate-08.gx
// (or the program alone on stdin: graphix-fuzz typemorph-one <outfile>)
// expected: typemorph: no flips, as with `let g = never()`, where the `open`
// set (typemorph.rs:297) keeps stmt-permute from swapping the two readers
// observed: exit 1,
// TYPEFLIP ... stmt-permute#1: N: type mismatch Array<fn(?#x: iN) -> iN> does
// not contain Array<'_N: fn(#x: iN) -> iN>
//   mutant: { let g = (never()); let b: ..fn(#x..) = [g]; let a: ..fn(?#x..) = [g]; .. }
// The refusal is the language rule (an unannotated let over bottom takes its
// type from its first use): `graphix --check` accepts this program and
// refuses the swapped one at `let a`. The same flip with the initializer
// `select 0 { _ => never() }`, `{ let z = 1; never() }`, and with
// `let xs = []` read as `= xs` (its element cell is decided the same way).
{ let g = (never()); let a: Array<fn(?#x: i64) -> i64> = [g]; let b: Array<fn(#x: i64) -> i64> = [g]; array::len(a) + array::len(b) }
